1
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:09,800
Example A: the lecture's graph, with every gradient still zero.

2
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:19,600
Base case: nudge L by h and L moves by h, so L.grad is 1.

3
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:24,400
A local derivative is how one node reacts to its own inputs.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,867
Predict: if d grows by 1, L changes by f. What number is that?

5
00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:42,667
The times node swaps in the other input: dL/dd is f, dL/df is d.

6
00:00:43,267 --> 00:00:52,067
Chain rule: rates multiply along a path, like a car and a bicycle.

7
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:57,533
The car is 8 times the man: multiply the two rates along the way.

8
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:15,600
The plus node is a router: it hands the gradient to both inputs.

9
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,733
Predict: e.grad is minus 2 and b is minus 3. What is a.grad?

10
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:42,600
Multiply along the path: minus 2 times 1 times minus 3 is 6.

11
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:52,000
Check by nudging a a tiny bit: L should move about 6 times as far.

12
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,400
One step: push every leaf a little along its own gradient.

13
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,600
Run the forward pass again: L rises from minus 8 to minus 7.2865.

14
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:10,200
Example B: what if we change the four inputs? Watch the numbers morph.

15
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:25,347
Set a = -2, b = 5, c = -3, f = 0.5. Every number is recomputed.

16
00:02:25,947 --> 00:02:30,497
L is now -6.5. Each gradient follows its own path of local derivatives.

17
00:02:31,097 --> 00:02:35,647
a.grad is e.grad times b: 0.5 times 5 is 2.5, down from 6.

18
00:02:36,247 --> 00:02:40,747
Now back to the notebook inputs, but with f = 0.

19
00:02:41,347 --> 00:02:59,227
L is multiplied by zero, so nothing upstream can matter.

20
00:02:59,827 --> 00:03:03,827
Only f.grad survives. This is why a dead branch stops learning.

21
00:03:04,427 --> 00:03:09,227
Expert corner: how do we know backprop is right? Check it live.

22
00:03:09,827 --> 00:03:14,027
For every leaf, nudge it both ways and measure how L reacts.

23
00:03:14,627 --> 00:03:21,727
Backprop and finite differences agree to about ten decimals.

24
00:03:22,327 --> 00:03:26,877
This costs two forward passes per leaf, so it is only a test.

25
00:03:27,477 --> 00:03:31,327
Reverse mode: one backward pass gives the slope for ALL inputs.

26
00:03:31,927 --> 00:03:40,393
Forward mode needs one pass per input. Watch them race.

27
00:03:40,993 --> 00:03:45,827
Millions of weights and one loss: that is why networks use reverse mode.

